---
[strong]A publish-subscribe server for tables. [/][text]You write Python functions that declare the tables they read and write; tabsdata versions the data, tracks the dependencies between the functions and runs them when their inputs change.[/]

[heading]Functions[/]
> [text]  A [term]publisher[/term] brings data in from outside, a [term]transformer[/term]
> derives tables from tables, and a [term]subscriber[/term] sends data out. Composed
> together they form a flow.[/]
---
[heading]Run it locally[/]
[command]  tdkserver quickstart [/][arrow]$arrow [/][text]Start a local instance in one step[/]
[command]  tdk login            [/][arrow]$arrow [/][text]Connect the client to it[/]
[command]  tdk --help           [/][arrow]$arrow [/][text]See every client command[/]
[text]  The instance listens on [/][link]http://127.0.0.1:2457[/][text].[/]
---
[heading]Elsewhere, or step by step[/]
> [text]  Add [term]--provider[/term] to choose where the instance runs: the default, [term]kminus[/term],
> needs nothing installed, while [term]eks[/term], [term]aks[/term] and [term]gke[/term] target managed Kubernetes on AWS,
> Azure and Google Cloud. Support for [term]docker[/term], on Docker Desktop, is coming soon.[/]

> [text]  The cloud providers need an existing cluster, a local kubeconfig whose current
> context points to it, and a namespace, already created, set in that context.[/]

> [text]  To adjust storage, database and other settings up front, run [term]tdkserver stubs[/term],
> fill in your provider's file in [term]~/.tabsdata/stubs[/term] and pass it to the
> quickstart as [term]--config @FILE[/term].[/]

[text]  To review the configuration before anything runs, split the quickstart:[/]
[command]  tdkserver init       [/][arrow]$arrow [/][text]Write an editable configuration[/]
[command]  tdkserver create     [/][arrow]$arrow [/][text]Create the instance from it[/]
[command]  tdkserver start      [/][arrow]$arrow [/][text]Start it[/]

